# goal Own the ACP conversation, and translate its failures into decisions

This is the only file that speaks ACP. Everything a caller needs to decide what to
do next — retry, fail over, give up — comes out of here as a status code, so a
mistranslation here is a routing mistake three systems away.

The session/process lifecycle, the option-ordering rule, the drain-after-cut rule
and the 429/502 split are stated in
[_.hint#lifecycle](../_.hint), [#option_reapply](../_.hint), [#cut_drain](../_.hint)
and [#limit_detection](../_.hint). This file records the contract.

# scope What this module owns

In: spawning the adapter, the ACP handshake, session lifecycle, config options, MCP
declaration, the prompt turn, cancellation, and mapping ACP failures to HTTP status.

Out: HTTP, OpenAI shapes, and any knowledge of what the caller asked for. `prompt`
receives ACP content blocks and a `limit` callback; it never sees a request body.

# func openSession {#open_session}

Opens an ACP session, declares the agent's MCP servers, and applies the agent's own
model/reasoning/options. Separate from `prompt` because the Responses API keeps a
session alive across requests; see
[responses.js.hint#no_replay](./responses.js.hint).

## result
`{id, active, options, agent}`. `options` is tracked on the session rather than
re-read from `session/new`, whose snapshot goes stale the moment an option is set —
a later turn reconfigures against the live set.

# func turn {#turn}

One turn inside an existing session. `overrides` re-applies config options first,
which is how a per-request `reasoning.effort` is honoured mid-conversation.

## invariant The abort listener is removed in `finally`
On a retained session the signal belongs to one request while the session serves
many, so a leaked listener cancels somebody else's turn.

# func prompt {#prompt}

One prompt turn on a fresh session: `openSession` + `turn` + `closeSession`. The
stateless path, and what chat completions uses.

## arg blocks
ACP `ContentBlock[]`, already translated.

## arg options.signal
Aborting it cancels the ACP turn. Removing the listener in `finally` is mandatory:
the signal outlives the turn and a leaked listener cancels the *next* one.

## arg options.onEvent
`{type: "text" | "reasoning" | "tool_call", ...}`, called as the agent streams and
always before this resolves.

## arg options.limit
See [openai.js.hint#limiter](./openai.js.hint). Optional.

## result
`{text, reasoning, stopReason, usage}`. `stopReason` is the limiter's verdict when
it fired, not the agent's `cancelled` — otherwise a truncation is reported as a
clean stop.

## error AgentError
The only error type leaving this module. Always carries `status` and `code`:
401 auth required, 429 usage limit, 499 cancelled, 400 unsupported option,
503 cannot spawn / shut down, 502 anything else.

## flow
1. Connect (memoized) — spawn, `initialize`, remember capabilities.
2. Build the session, declaring the agent's MCP servers.
3. Apply model, then reasoning, then raw options — re-reading the option set each time.
4. Prompt; consume updates, consulting `limit` after each text chunk.
5. On cut: cancel, then drain to the stop message without accumulating.
6. Always: remove the abort listener, dispose, and `session/close` when supported.

# invariant tool_call updates are progress, never content {#tool_calls_are_progress}

The agent's own tool calls are surfaced through `onEvent` and never appended to
`text`. They are things the agent already did — not OpenAI `tool_calls` for the
caller to execute and answer. Anything that makes them look like the latter is
wrong; see [_.hint#mcp_tools](../_.hint).

# rule This module routes session updates itself {#own_routing}

`#sinks` maps a session id to the turn currently reading it, filled by one
`session/update` handler on the client app; `turn()` sends `session/prompt` as a
plain request and returns when it resolves.

The SDK's `ActiveSession` does exactly this and cannot be used, because the only
way to obtain one is `session/new`: its constructor and `attachSession` are both
private. A session restored with `session/resume` would therefore have no way to
receive its updates — which would rule out parking, reviving and forking
altogether. Twenty lines here buys the whole session lifecycle.

Two properties this relies on, both true of the SDK's dispatch and neither obvious:

- **Order.** Notifications and the response travel the same stream and are handled
  in order, so everything the agent sent before answering has already run by the
  time the request resolves.
- **Nothing after the answer.** Updates arriving once the turn has ended belong to
  no turn and are dropped — the sink is removed in `finally` precisely so a late
  chunk cannot land in the NEXT turn's accumulator. This matches what the SDK's
  queue did: `nextUpdate()` also stopped at the prompt response.

# rule A warm base is forked, never used {#warm_base}

`warmup.prompt` opens one session, runs that prompt in it, and every conversation
afterwards is a `session/fork` of it. What that buys is the re-orientation a cold
session pays before it can do anything — reading the project's instructions,
listing the tree, grepping for its bearings — which every new conversation
otherwise pays again.

Four things hold this together, and each of them is load-bearing:

- **The base never serves a turn.** It is forked, and the fork is what the caller
  gets. Prompting the base directly would put one conversation's history into
  every conversation started after it.
- **Single-flight.** Two requests arriving cold must not warm two bases and leave
  one resident with nothing pointing at it. `#base.warming` is the promise both
  wait on.
- **Every failure falls back to cold.** No fork capability, a warm-up that throws,
  a fork that is refused — all of them open an ordinary session. Starting cold is
  slower and more expensive and it is never WRONG, which makes it the only correct
  fallback. A failed warm-up is also not cached: the next request tries again.
- **Options are re-applied to the fork.** The protocol does not promise a fork
  carries the parent's selections, and two cheap local RPCs beat a thread silently
  running on the wrong model.

**It costs a real turn of a real subscription**, once per `ttlMs`, plus one
resident session for as long as the agent lives. It is worth having when
conversations start often enough to amortise that, and not otherwise — hence no
default.

`ttlMs` is a bet the operator makes. A base that read the repository is stale the
moment the repository moves, and nothing here can know when that happened.

# invariant A dead child never takes the server down {#respawn}

The `exit` handler drops the memoized connection so the next request respawns rather
than writing into a dead pipe. `close()` is terminal in the other direction: after
it, `prompt` throws 503 instead of spawning a CLI nothing is left to reap.

# bad Never widen the 429 net to catch an unfamiliar error

An unrecognised failure stays 502. A router that reads 429 spends its next provider;
doing that on a crash it should have retried burns a subscription for nothing. New
phrasings belong in `server.limitPatterns` (config), not in this file.

# test agent

- a turn streams reasoning, tool calls and text in order, and reports usage
- model and reasoning are selected by category, not by option id
- raw `options` address an id directly, including booleans
- an unavailable model value, and an unknown option id, are 400 — never a fallback
- selecting a model that removes the reasoning selector reports *that*, not a stale id
- a model with no reasoning selector works when none is requested
- quota phrasing is 429; an unrelated failure is 502; `limitPatterns` are configurable
- aborting the signal cancels the turn in the agent
- `max_tokens` and `stop` cut the turn, and nothing past the cut is emitted or accumulated
- `mcpServers` reach `session/new` in ACP's array shape
- the child is reused across turns; after `close()` a further prompt is 503
- an unspawnable command is 503, not a crash
